We interpret constants as objects, unary predicates as classes of objects with a property and binary predicates as the relation between objects. 
Intepretation as graphs : We can represent this interpretation graphically by using arrows between objects for binary predicates and labelling and object for unary predicates. 

Predicate rules : 
A predicate rule is a predicate clause such that all variable in the head appear in the body
A Predicate knowledge base  is a set of predicate rules.

Models : 
Whenever the body of an interpretation function is true then its head is true. 
I is a model of the KB if it satisfies all rules in K
K ⊨ P(𝑎) if P(𝑎) is true in all models of K

Knowledge propagation : we do this by building a minimal representation of all possible models (canonical model)
We are trying to find the things that follow when our kb is true and finding all possible models for our KB

If we can conclude something is true in the canonical model then it is true for all possible models

This canonical model can be queried to decide any entailments (conclusions)

The canonical model solely depends on the objects, if we add any new models it wont be a model anymore. 

Constructing the canonical model : 

The canonical model is built from and empty model by adding only the necessities to satisfy the constraints of the model. 
1.	Create all relevant nodes : We assume each constant refers to a different element of the domain
2.	Add facts 
3.	Propagate rules : If we can make the body of our rule true then we add everything needed to satisfy the head, if the body is true then the head is also true. 

We define the domain of the canonical model as all the constants of the KB. They are interpreted as themselves. 

For each fact P(a) ← add the label P to the node a 
For each fact Q(a,b) ← add a Q labelled edge from a to b

Take a predicate rule, if all those predicated are true then the predicate in the head must be true. 
Substitute the variables with constraints, then they are facts. Look at all possible substitutions. 

